  "nameStory": "The 'Yellow Unicorn' gets its magical name from the sharp, pointed bump (called an umbo) that sits right in the center of its cap like a unicorn's horn. This distinctive horn-like projection makes it stand out among the many pinkgill mushrooms, earning it this whimsical but descriptive common name that's easy to remember once you spot that telltale point.",
  "description": "The Yellow Unicorn Entoloma is a small to medium-sized mushroom that's been calling New England home since at least 1859. It's easily recognized by its bright yellow cap topped with a sharp, cone-like point that gives it its unicorn nickname. Like all pinkgills, its gills start out whitish but develop a distinctive pink color as the spores mature. This ground-dwelling fungus prefers the moist soils of our region's forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Sharp, pointed umbo (bump) in the center of a conical, bright yellow cap that looks like a tiny wizard's hat",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Start whitish when young but turn pink as spores mature, a hallmark of all pinkgill mushrooms",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Pink spore print - place cap gill-side down on white paper overnight to see this diagnostic feature",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Typically yellowish and fibrous, without any ring or bulbous base",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat",
      "description": "Always growing directly from soil in wet forest areas, never on wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Yellow Unicorn Entoloma in wet coniferous and deciduous forests throughout New England, where it grows directly from the rich, moist soil. It thrives in areas with good moisture retention, often appearing after periods of rain in both pine-dominated areas and mixed hardwood forests. The fungus seems to prefer the acidic soils common under conifers but adapts well to various forest floor conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early fruiting may begin in late spring during wet periods, especially in lower elevation forests where soil warms first.",
    "summer": "Peak season for finding fresh specimens, particularly after summer rains when soil moisture is high and conditions are ideal.",
    "fall": "Continued fruiting through early fall, often producing some of the best specimens as autumn rains return to the forest.",
    "winter": "Not active during New England's cold season, though old specimens may persist as dried remnants."
  },
  "funFact": "This little mushroom was one of the first fungi scientifically described from New England back in 1859, making it practically a founding member of our region's documented fungal community - it's been quietly sporting its unicorn horn in our forests for over 160 years of recorded history!",
  "confusionNotes": "Other yellow Entoloma species can look similar, but the sharp, prominent umbo is your key - if it doesn't have that distinct unicorn horn point, it's probably a different species. Memory trick: 'Unicorn = pointed horn on top' - no point, no unicorn!",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite the charming 'pinkgill' family name, never assume these mushrooms are edible - this species is inedible and potentially poisonous. The Entoloma genus is notoriously difficult to identify definitively without microscopic examination, so this is strictly a 'look but don't touch' group for foragers.",
  "compareHints": "Among its many Entoloma relatives in New England, the Yellow Unicorn stands out with its bright yellow color and that signature sharp umbo - most other pinkgills lack this pronounced pointed bump. The Aborted Entoloma is much more common but appears white and often has a deformed, lumpy appearance, while other colorful species like the violet pinkgill are distinctly purple rather than yellow.",
